+# Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope; Free-Text Veterinarian Search Is Not
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian. Its reason was that owner search demonstrates paged prefix search once, and repeating it elsewhere adds surface without adding understanding.
+
+That row was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey. Whether it was a deliberate decision or a survey artifact stayed open, so a request touching it could be read as reopening it by implication.
+
+The product owner asked on 2026-08-08 for the veterinarian directory to be narrowable by specialty. The request sits against NG-9. It also sits against the 2026-07-31 withdrawal of REQ-VET-002, which retired the machine-readable veterinarian list as an implementation artifact with no consumer.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Read NG-9 as barring the request.** Rejected: the owner narrowed the row explicitly rather than leaving it to interpretation.
+2. **Drop NG-9 entirely.** Rejected: free-text search over veterinarians still repeats what owner search already teaches, and the owner kept it out.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to free-text search, admitting a filter on an attribute the directory already shows** (chosen).
+4. **Ship a visible control alongside the filter.** Rejected here: the owner scoped this request to the address contract and left a control to a later request. Recorded as NG-10.
+5. **Reuse REQ-VET-002 for the reinstated machine-readable list.** Rejected: a withdrawn ID is never reused, so links to the withdrawal keep resolving.
+
+## Decision
+
+Three product decisions, quoted from the owner (2026-08-08).
+
+> Narrow non-goal NG-9: free-text veterinarian search stays out of scope, but filtering the directory by an attribute it already shows (specialty) is in scope. Record the narrowing the way the project records non-goal changes.
+
+> Reinstate the `/vets` JSON endpoint as a supported surface, with this specialty filter as its first requested capability. Mint a fresh requirement id for it — the withdrawn REQ-VET-002 stays withdrawn and its id is not reused.
+
+> This is a URL-contract-only feature: neither surface gains a form, dropdown, or other page control in this request (a visible control may come as a follow-up request). Pagination links carry the `specialty` parameter so filtered pages stay navigable.
+
+NG-9 is narrowed to free-text search. NG-10 records the absent page control. REQ-VET-003 carries the machine-readable directory as a supported surface, and REQ-VET-004 carries the specialty filter across both surfaces.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open provenance consultation as a decided, narrowed row. Every other undecided row remains part of it.
+- NG-10 is a deferral the owner named, not a permanent refusal. A later request for a visible control narrows it with its own recorded decision.
+- REQ-VET-002 stays withdrawn and its ID stays retired. Existing links to the withdrawal keep resolving.
+- The machine-readable directory gains a requirement without gaining a named consumer. That gap is recorded as an open question.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9, NG-10
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 and the new NG-10.
+- [REQ-VET-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the machine-readable directory as a supported surface.
+- [REQ-VET-004](../prd.md#req-vet-004) — the specialty filter across both surfaces.
